[528,2,0,1898,"An Icy Night, New York","Alfred Stieglitz photographed bare trees and a path by streetlights in a freezing night. It took long exposures and patience, and the soft glow makes the park look like a winter dream.","What sounds would you hear in a park on a freezing night?"],
[533,1,0,1898,"Mount of the Holy Cross, Colorado","William Henry Jackson photographed this peak, where snow fills the cracks in the shape of a cross. In the 1890s his company used a Swiss process called photochrom to add color to black-and-white photographs, and postcards like this were a sensation.","Find the cross on the mountain."],
[534,0,0,1898,"Geronimo","Geronimo was an Apache leader who fought for years to protect his people's homeland. At the time of this portrait, he was a prisoner of war and was taken to an exposition in Omaha, Nebraska, where he was photographed.","What do you read in his face?"],
[535,3,0,1908,"Ivey Mill, Hickory, North Carolina","Lewis Hine photographed children working in cotton mills across the American South. This tiny girl stands among the huge spinning machines. His pictures helped convince Americans to pass laws against child labor.","How old do you think she is? How would you feel working in this mill?"],
[536,0,0,1899,"Blessed Art Thou Among Women","Gertrude Käsebier posed a mother in white and her daughter in dark clothes in a doorway. The picture, named for a line from the Bible, made her famous when it was shown at galleries.","Why do you think one is dressed in white and the other in black?"],
[539,0,0,1899,"The Manger","Käsebier's The Manger shows a mother and child bathed in soft light, like a modern Madonna. She believed photographers could make pictures as moving as paintings.","How do the soft light and white dress change the mood?"],
[543,3,0,1899,"The Bread Carrier","A woman carries a big basket of bread on a Paris street. Eugène Atget photographed street workers, and the pictures became a record of old Parisian jobs.","What other jobs on the street do you know about?"],
[545,3,0,1899,"The Lampshade Seller","A man walks a Paris lane with lampshades piled on his arm. Atget photographed people who sold things on the street and sold his prints to artists, who used them as references.","How many lampshades can you see?"],
[548,7,0,1899,"Nikola Tesla in His Laboratory","Nikola Tesla sits calmly while artificial lightning crackles from his giant coil in Colorado Springs. The photograph is a double exposure: he sat in one shot, and the sparks were added in another, since he would not have survived real lightning that close.","Why do you think they used a double exposure?"],
[552,5,0,1900,"Sun Dance Encampment, Piegan","Edward S. Curtis photographed a huge circle of tipis on the prairie during a Piegan sun dance gathering. He spent thirty years documenting Native American communities.","Why would so many families gather together on the prairie?"],
[553,1,0,1900,"Stirling Castle","James Craig Annan, a Scottish photographer, made soft, dark prints of the castle on its rock. He used a printing method called photogravure, which gave rich, velvet-like tones.","How does the castle look against the sky?"],
[554,2,0,1900,"The Street, Fifth Avenue","In a gentle snowstorm, horse-drawn carriages move along Fifth Avenue in New York. Stieglitz made the picture soft, like a painting, and printed it in a way that glowed.","What is moving along the street? What isn't moving?"],
[555,8,0,1900,"Spring Showers","A slim young tree stands on a wet New York street in a soft mist of rain. Stieglitz loved to photograph weather, and he used it to make the picture look like a drawing.","Does it feel like a happy rain or a sad one?"],
[560,8,0,1902,"Rodin: The Thinker","Edward Steichen photographed the sculptor Auguste Rodin in front of his famous statue The Thinker. In the dark, Rodin's shadow seems to join the statue.","Which one is the sculpture and which is the sculptor?"],
[566,0,0,1900,"Amos Two Bulls","Gertrude Käsebier photographed members of Buffalo Bill's Wild West show when they came to New York. Amos Two Bulls, a Lakota man, is shown in a dignified profile, in a picture that looks like a painting.","What do you think he is looking at?"],
[575,2,0,1902,"The Hand of Man","A steam locomotive moves out of a rail yard in New York in smoky light. Stieglitz showed that machines and cities could be as beautiful as landscapes.","What do you hear when a train passes?"],
